        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
NASA currently requires a physician-trained Crew Medical Officer (CMO) for missions exceeding 210 days in length. This is based on the nominal 180-day length of an International Space Station (ISS) mission, which affords relatively quick and easy return to Earth for treating complicated cases.  As NASA considers various Design Reference Missions (DRMs) for exploration, there are DRMs of only a few weeks duration that could not quickly return to Earth. Accordingly, they may well carry a medical risk level high enough to warrant the judgment of a physician-trained CMO. In contrast, the current assumption that missions to Mars would be less medically risky with a physician-trained CMO may be invalid. Due to rigorous preflight screening, which lessens the likelihood of a disease state, and the limited nature of the envisioned medical kits, perhaps NASA's treatment options are limited enough that the judgment of a physician-trained CMO would not appreciably reduce medical mission risk. This study had the following specific aims:<ol>
<li>To develop a Medical Judgment Pathway Metric (MJPM) for assessing clinical judgment based on evidenced-based practice for treating two common conditions from the Exploration Medical Condition List (EMCL).
<li>To validate the MJPM by a multi-disciplinary expert medical team to confirm principal components of the MJPM. 
<li>To test the MJPM in an analog population using populations that mirror those of the astronaut corps in terms of both age and training.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
This investigation was completed by Summa Health Systems with advice from the Integrated Medical Model (IMM) team about the suitability of the metric for modeling. This study achieved two aims: 1. Develop a MJPM assessing clinical judgment; and 2. Demonstrate evidence of validity and reliability of MJPM scores. The individual MJPM items, domains, and sections of the MJPM were built based on existing standardized frameworks. Content validity was determined by a convenient sample of eight experts. The MJPM instrument was pilot tested in four medical simulations with a team of three medical raters assessing 40 participants with four levels of medical experience and skill.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Raters were highly consistent in their MJPM scores in each scenario as well as their evaluation of the expected patient outcome. For each simulation scenario, average rater score cut-scores significantly predicted expected loss of life or stabilization. 
<p>
